WEBVTT
NOTE why-is-carbon-fiber-so-strong - timed from measured narration audio

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.392
Carbon fiber composites reach tensile
strengths between 3 and 7 gigapascals.

00:00:05.392 --> 00:00:08.229
Steel tops out around half a gigapascal.

00:00:08.229 --> 00:00:13.073
That factor alone does not explain why
aerospace engineers specify carbon fiber

00:00:13.073 --> 00:00:14.421
for primary structure.

00:00:14.421 --> 00:00:16.735
The strength-to-weight ratio does.

00:00:16.735 --> 00:00:21.703
Carbon fiber composites deliver that
strength at one fifth the density of

00:00:21.703 --> 00:00:26.944
steel, and the reason traces to a bond
length of 0.142 nanometers and the way

00:00:26.944 --> 00:00:29.189
those bonds organize into planes.

00:00:29.189 --> 00:00:31.264
Why is carbon fiber so strong?

00:00:31.264 --> 00:00:36.610
A single covalent carbon-carbon bond in a
graphite plane sustains roughly 5.9

00:00:36.610 --> 00:00:39.248
gigapascals of stress before breaking.

00:00:39.248 --> 00:00:43.670
An iron-iron metallic bond in steel
sustains about 2.8 gigapascals.

00:00:43.670 --> 00:00:48.224
The carbon bond is more than twice as
strong, and it is also shorter.

00:00:48.224 --> 00:00:52.758
The bond length in graphite is 0.142
nanometers.

00:00:52.758 --> 00:00:57.576
In steel the iron-iron spacing is 0.248
nanometers.

00:00:57.576 --> 00:01:02.118
Shorter bonds mean more bonds per unit
length, and more bonds mean more

00:01:02.118 --> 00:01:04.932
load-bearing paths across the same
distance.

00:01:04.932 --> 00:01:08.131
The strength advantage begins at the
atomic scale.

00:01:08.131 --> 00:01:11.546
Metallic bonds distribute electrons across
a lattice.

00:01:11.546 --> 00:01:15.283
Covalent bonds localize electron pairs
between two nuclei.

00:01:15.283 --> 00:01:19.811
That localization concentrates the
electrostatic attraction, and the bond

00:01:19.811 --> 00:01:21.485
resists being pulled apart.

00:01:21.485 --> 00:01:26.514
Steel's metallic lattice allows atoms to
slide past one another under shear stress,

00:01:26.514 --> 00:01:30.088
which is why steel deforms plastically
before it fractures.

00:01:30.088 --> 00:01:35.299
Carbon in a graphite plane does not slide.
The bonds break, or they hold.

00:01:35.299 --> 00:01:39.574
Carbon atoms in a graphite plane adopt sp2
hybridization.

00:01:39.574 --> 00:01:45.651
Three of the four valence electrons form
sigma bonds in a flat hexagonal lattice.

00:01:45.651 --> 00:01:50.467
The fourth electron occupies a pi orbital
perpendicular to the plane.

00:01:50.467 --> 00:01:55.843
Those pi electrons delocalize across the
sheet, but they do not contribute to

00:01:55.843 --> 00:01:57.029
tensile strength.

00:01:57.029 --> 00:01:58.183
The sigma bonds do.

00:01:58.183 --> 00:02:02.435
Within the plane, strength is
isotropic—pull in any direction parallel

00:02:02.435 --> 00:02:06.261
to the sheet and you are pulling against
the same bond network.

00:02:06.261 --> 00:02:10.480
Between planes, only weak van der Waals
forces hold the sheets together.

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:15.675
Graphite flakes apart easily because those
interplanar forces are three orders of

00:02:15.675 --> 00:02:18.304
magnitude weaker than the in-plane bonds.

00:02:18.304 --> 00:02:23.206
The theoretical strength of a perfect
graphite plane exceeds 100 gigapascals.

00:02:23.206 --> 00:02:27.790
That figure comes from calculations of the
force required to break every

00:02:27.790 --> 00:02:31.419
carbon-carbon bond across a cross-section
simultaneously.

00:02:31.419 --> 00:02:34.076
Real carbon fiber never reaches that
number.

00:02:34.076 --> 00:02:39.029
Defects, misalignments and surface flaws
reduce the realized strength by more than

00:02:39.029 --> 00:02:40.358
an order of magnitude.

00:02:40.358 --> 00:02:44.405
The gap between theory and practice is
where manufacturing matters.

00:02:44.405 --> 00:02:49.546
Carbon fiber is made by heating a polymer
precursor, usually polyacrylonitrile,

00:02:49.546 --> 00:02:51.824
through a series of thermal stages.

00:02:51.824 --> 00:02:57.367
Oxidation at 200 to 300 degrees Celsius
stabilizes the polymer chain.

00:02:57.367 --> 00:03:03.713
Carbonization at 1000 to 1500 degrees
Celsius drives off hydrogen, nitrogen and

00:03:03.713 --> 00:03:06.123
oxygen, leaving mostly carbon.

00:03:06.123 --> 00:03:11.573
Graphitization at temperatures above 2000
degrees Celsius encourages the carbon

00:03:11.573 --> 00:03:16.955
atoms to reorganize into stacked graphite
planes aligned along the fiber axis.

00:03:16.955 --> 00:03:18.656
The alignment is not perfect.

00:03:18.656 --> 00:03:22.763
Defects remain, and those defects limit
the fiber's realized strength.

00:03:22.763 --> 00:03:27.695
A perfect graphite whisker can exceed 100
gigapascals in laboratory conditions.

00:03:27.695 --> 00:03:32.503
Commercial carbon fiber reaches 3 to 7
gigapascals in a composite because the

00:03:32.503 --> 00:03:36.187
fiber contains misaligned regions, voids
and surface flaws.

00:03:36.187 --> 00:03:40.301
The processing temperature determines the
degree of graphitization.

00:03:40.301 --> 00:03:44.783
Higher temperatures produce better
alignment and higher modulus, but they

00:03:44.783 --> 00:03:47.915
also cost more and require longer
processing times.

00:03:47.915 --> 00:03:52.874
Aerospace-grade fiber is typically
processed above 2000 degrees Celsius.

00:03:52.874 --> 00:03:56.731
Industrial-grade fiber may stop at 1500
degrees Celsius.

00:03:56.731 --> 00:04:01.599
The trade-off is between performance and
cost, and the choice depends on the

00:04:01.599 --> 00:04:02.368
application.

00:04:02.368 --> 00:04:06.274
A pressure vessel for a rocket needs the
highest grade.

00:04:06.274 --> 00:04:08.901
A sporting goods application may not.

00:04:08.901 --> 00:04:11.000
Carbon fiber by itself is brittle.

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:14.272
It fractures under bending or impact
without warning.

00:04:14.272 --> 00:04:19.549
The composite combines fiber with a
polymer resin matrix, usually epoxy.

00:04:19.549 --> 00:04:25.632
The resin transfers load between fibers,
fills voids and protects the fiber surface

00:04:25.632 --> 00:04:26.512
from damage.

00:04:26.512 --> 00:04:30.366
The resin also limits the composite's
temperature ceiling.

00:04:30.366 --> 00:04:33.024
Epoxy softens above 120 degrees Celsius.

00:04:33.024 --> 00:04:38.686
The fiber itself remains stable to 600
degrees Celsius in an inert atmosphere,

00:04:38.686 --> 00:04:40.645
but the matrix fails first.

00:04:40.645 --> 00:04:46.088
High-temperature composites use polyimide
or ceramic matrices, and those systems

00:04:46.088 --> 00:04:49.013
require different processing and cost
more.

00:04:49.013 --> 00:04:53.982
When you pull on a carbon fiber composite,
the matrix distributes the load to

00:04:53.982 --> 00:04:55.144
individual fibers.

00:04:55.144 --> 00:05:00.376
Each fiber carries the load along its
axis, where the graphite planes are

00:05:00.376 --> 00:05:05.832
If a fiber breaks, the matrix
redistributes that fiber's load to its

00:05:05.832 --> 00:05:11.236
The composite's tensile strength depends
on fiber volume fraction, fiber alignment,

00:05:11.236 --> 00:05:14.883
matrix properties and the bond between
fiber and matrix.

00:05:14.883 --> 00:05:19.644
Aerospace-grade prepreg typically contains
60 percent fiber by volume.

00:05:19.644 --> 00:05:25.154
Higher fiber content increases strength
but makes the material harder to form and

00:05:25.154 --> 00:05:26.515
more prone to voids.

00:05:26.515 --> 00:05:32.088
What interests me is the translation
layer. An instrument doesn't hand us

00:05:32.088 --> 00:05:37.288
It produces a signal, and then humans
calibrate it, process it, interpret it.

00:05:37.288 --> 00:05:41.986
Steel is isotropic. Its strength is the
same in every direction.

00:05:41.986 --> 00:05:44.923
Carbon fiber composites are anisotropic.

00:05:44.923 --> 00:05:49.835
Strength is highest along the fiber
direction and much lower perpendicular to

00:05:49.835 --> 00:05:55.877
A unidirectional laminate might have 1500
megapascals tensile strength along the

00:05:55.877 --> 00:05:58.747
fibers and 50 megapascals across them.

00:05:58.747 --> 00:06:03.005
That thirty-to-one ratio means the
designer must know the load paths and

00:06:03.005 --> 00:06:04.779
orient the fibers accordingly.

00:06:04.779 --> 00:06:10.242
Aerospace laminates stack plies at
different angles—zero, plus and minus 45,

00:06:10.242 --> 00:06:13.189
and 90 degrees—to balance in-plane loads.

00:06:13.189 --> 00:06:17.861
The stacking sequence is part of the
design, not an afterthought.

00:06:17.861 --> 00:06:23.221
You cannot design a carbon fiber part the
way you design a steel part.

00:06:23.221 --> 00:06:25.530
The material is not a drop-in replacement.

00:06:25.530 --> 00:06:29.927
You must account for anisotropy, for the
resin's temperature limit, for the fact

00:06:29.927 --> 00:06:32.731
that the composite does not yield before
it breaks.

00:06:32.731 --> 00:06:37.558
You also must account for moisture
absorption, ultraviolet degradation and

00:06:37.558 --> 00:06:40.885
galvanic corrosion if the composite
contacts metal.

00:06:40.885 --> 00:06:42.576
These are not minor details.

00:06:42.576 --> 00:06:47.408
They are the difference between a part
that works and one that fails in service.

00:06:47.408 --> 00:06:49.713
The fiber-matrix interface is critical.

00:06:49.713 --> 00:06:54.204
If the bond between fiber and resin is
weak, the matrix cannot transfer load

00:06:54.204 --> 00:06:56.864
effectively, and the composite
underperforms.

00:06:56.864 --> 00:07:01.676
Surface treatments—oxidation, sizing,
plasma treatment—improve adhesion.

00:07:01.676 --> 00:07:07.089
The treatments add cost and complexity,
but without them the composite's strength

00:07:07.089 --> 00:07:08.827
drops by 20 to 40 percent.

00:07:08.827 --> 00:07:13.654
The interface is invisible in the finished
part, but it determines whether the part

00:07:13.654 --> 00:07:14.933
meets its design load.

00:07:14.933 --> 00:07:20.240
Carbon fiber composites deliver specific
tensile strengths around 1800 megapascals

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:22.117
per kilogram per cubic meter.

00:07:22.117 --> 00:07:25.238
Steel delivers 130. Aluminum delivers 260.

00:07:25.238 --> 00:07:31.181
The composite is seven times better than
aluminum and fourteen times better than

00:07:31.181 --> 00:07:31.627
steel.

00:07:31.627 --> 00:07:36.901
That advantage compounds in structures
where weight savings reduce the load on

00:07:36.901 --> 00:07:38.051
other components.

00:07:38.051 --> 00:07:42.543
An aircraft wing made from carbon fiber
weighs less, so the fuselage can be

00:07:42.543 --> 00:07:46.078
lighter, so the engines can be smaller, so
fuel burn drops.

00:07:46.078 --> 00:07:49.672
The system-level benefit exceeds the
material-level benefit.

00:07:49.672 --> 00:07:50.969
The cost is high.

00:07:50.969 --> 00:07:56.845
Carbon fiber prepreg costs 30 to 150
dollars per kilogram depending on grade.

00:07:56.845 --> 00:08:00.128
Steel costs less than 1 dollar per
kilogram.

00:08:00.128 --> 00:08:03.336
Aluminum costs 2 to 5 dollars per
kilogram.

00:08:03.336 --> 00:08:08.788
The composite also requires controlled
curing—autoclaves, vacuum bagging, precise

00:08:08.788 --> 00:08:12.760
temperature ramps. A steel part can be
welded in the field.

00:08:12.760 --> 00:08:17.023
A carbon fiber part cannot. Damage
tolerance is lower.

00:08:17.023 --> 00:08:19.944
A dent in aluminum might be cosmetic.

00:08:19.944 --> 00:08:24.613
A crack in a composite laminate can
propagate between plies without visible

00:08:24.613 --> 00:08:25.547
surface damage.

00:08:25.547 --> 00:08:30.029
Inspection requires ultrasound or
thermography, not just a visual check.

00:08:30.029 --> 00:08:33.629
The economic case for carbon fiber depends
on the application.

00:08:33.629 --> 00:08:38.507
In aerospace, where fuel savings over the
life of the aircraft can reach millions of

00:08:38.507 --> 00:08:40.829
dollars, the material cost is justified.

00:08:40.829 --> 00:08:45.053
In automotive applications, where
production volumes are higher and cost

00:08:45.053 --> 00:08:48.045
sensitivity is greater, carbon fiber
remains niche.

00:08:48.045 --> 00:08:50.317
The material is not universally better.

00:08:50.317 --> 00:08:54.626
It is better where weight matters more
than cost, and where the design can

00:08:54.626 --> 00:08:55.965
accommodate anisotropy.

00:08:55.965 --> 00:09:01.587
The numbers in this script trace to NIST
for bond-level data, ASM International for

00:09:01.587 --> 00:09:07.140
composite properties and MIT's Department
of Materials Science and Engineering for

00:09:07.140 --> 00:09:07.885
processing.

00:09:07.885 --> 00:09:13.208
The bond strength of 5.9 gigapascals for
carbon-carbon in graphite comes from

00:09:13.208 --> 00:09:15.005
NIST's materials database.

00:09:15.005 --> 00:09:20.585
The composite tensile range of 3 to 7
gigapascals comes from ASM International's

00:09:20.585 --> 00:09:22.189
handbook on composites.

00:09:22.189 --> 00:09:27.073
The specific strength comparison comes
from published density and strength tables

00:09:27.073 --> 00:09:28.701
available from both bodies.

00:09:28.701 --> 00:09:34.770
The processing temperatures of 200 to 300
degrees Celsius for oxidation, 1000 to

00:09:34.770 --> 00:09:40.384
1500 degrees Celsius for carbonization,
and above 2000 degrees Celsius for

00:09:40.384 --> 00:09:45.997
graphitization come from MIT DMSE's open
courseware on polymer composites.

00:09:45.997 --> 00:09:52.062
The tensile strength range of 3 to 7
gigapascals is wide because it depends on

00:09:52.062 --> 00:09:57.893
fiber grade, resin properties, fiber
volume fraction, void content and cure

00:09:57.893 --> 00:09:58.515
quality.

00:09:58.515 --> 00:10:02.980
A research-grade prepreg cured in an
autoclave reaches the high end.

00:10:02.980 --> 00:10:06.264
An industrial-grade wet layup reaches the
low end.

00:10:06.264 --> 00:10:08.290
The published range is honest.

00:10:08.290 --> 00:10:13.018
Tighter bounds require specifying the
system in detail, and even then,

00:10:13.018 --> 00:10:15.179
batch-to-batch variation exists.

00:10:15.179 --> 00:10:21.306
The bond strength of 5.9 gigapascals is a
calculated value for an ideal graphite

00:10:21.306 --> 00:10:21.765
plane.

00:10:21.765 --> 00:10:25.486
Real fibers contain defects, so real
strength is lower.

00:10:25.486 --> 00:10:30.155
The evidence tells you what is possible
and what limits the possible.

00:10:30.155 --> 00:10:34.935
Carbon fiber is strong because
carbon-carbon bonds are strong, because

00:10:34.935 --> 00:10:40.261
those bonds organize into planes, and
because the planes align along the fiber

00:10:40.261 --> 00:10:40.603
axis.

00:10:40.603 --> 00:10:44.739
The composite is strong where the fibers
run, and only there.

00:10:44.739 --> 00:10:50.435
The strength-to-weight ratio justifies the
cost in applications where weight matters

00:10:50.435 --> 00:10:53.690
enough to pay for it. The material is not
magic.

00:10:53.690 --> 00:10:56.267
It is chemistry and geometry, aligned.
